-- title:
--   The scale step, the lattice graph, and the path theorem as a statement
-- statement:
--   Fix a dimension $d$, a minimum apex angle $\vartheta$, a separation threshold $R_0$, and a number $L$ of reference cones. A **scale step** is a natural number $\Delta$ that is even, exceeds a geometric constant $\delta = \delta(d,\vartheta)$ coming from the renormalisation lemma, exceeds $R_0$, and is divisible by $L$ — the conditions under which each town at a power of $\Delta$ is sparsely populated.
--
--   The **lattice graph** $G$ of a configuration $\Gamma$ has the lattice points of $\mathbb{Z}^d \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ as vertices, with $x$ and $y$ adjacent when both are lattice points, they are distinct, and one lies in the other's cone:
--
--   $$x \sim y \iff x, y \in \mathbb{Z}^d \ \text{ and }\ \big(y \in V^\Gamma[x] \ \text{ or }\ x \in V^\Gamma[y]\big).$$
--
--   This relation is symmetric and irreflexive (irreflexivity because a configuration puts no loop at a point), so it is a simple graph; the lattice is also packaged as a subtype.
--
--   The module then records the target theorem as a proposition. Given $\Gamma$ and constants $N, M \in \mathbb{N}$ and $\lambda \in \mathbb{R}$, the **path property** asserts the existence of a family of walks $p_{xy}$ in $G$, one for each ordered pair of lattice points $x, y$ and running from $x$ to $y$, such that
--
--   $$\mathrm{length}(p_{xy}) \le N, \qquad \#\{(x,y) : e \in \mathrm{edges}(p_{xy})\} \le M \ \text{ for every edge } e, \qquad \lambda^{-1}\|x-y\| \le \|u - w\| \le \lambda \|x-y\|$$
--
--   for every dart $(u,w)$ of $p_{xy}$. The first clause bounds the length of every path, the second bounds the number of pairs whose path traverses a given edge, and the third says every edge has length comparable to the distance between the endpoints of the pair. Finally, the theorem itself is the statement that for the given $d$, $\vartheta$, $R_0$ there exist $N, M > 0$ and $\lambda \ge R_0$ such that *every* $\vartheta$-bounded configuration on $\mathbb{R}^d$ has the path property with those constants.
--
--   Putting the theorem in this form makes the quantifier order explicit — the constants are produced before the configuration is mentioned, which is precisely the assertion that they depend only on $d$, $\vartheta$ and $R_0$ — and makes claims (2)–(4) expressible at all: a reflexive–transitive connectivity relation records that a path exists but not which one, whereas a `SimpleGraph.Walk` carries a length, a list of darts and a list of edges.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Multiplicity is measured by `Set.encard` in $\mathbb{N}^{\infty}$, so the bound $M$ constrains a possibly infinite set. Edge lengths are stated over darts (oriented edges) so that the two endpoints can be named; the length comparison is two-sided and uses $\lambda^{-1}$, which is junk when $\lambda = 0$, ruled out by $\lambda \ge R_0$ at the use sites where $R_0 > 0$.
